  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "False positives and rapid tests put the rate of infection near zero.",
      "sourceText": "False positives and rapid tests put the rate of infection near zero",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 67,
      "role": "CORE"
    },
    {
      "id": "atom-2",
      "text": "False positives and rapid tests put the rate of infection near zero and that there will be no second wave of infections.",
      "sourceText": "there will be no second wave of infections",
      "start": 77,
      "end": 119,
      "role": "CORE"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-39",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Tests gave false positive results for 1% of those without COVID-19.\n",
          "start": 4482,
          "end": 4550,
          "context": "Tests that specifically detected IgG or IgM antibodies were the most accurate and, when testing people from 21 days after first symptoms, they detected 93% of people with COVID-19.  Tests gave false positive results for 1% of those without COVID-19.\n Below we illustrate results for two different scenarios.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-45",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Of these, 2 people (0.2%) would actually have COVID-19 (false negative result).\n",
          "start": 4895,
          "end": 4975,
          "context": "- 974 people would test negative.  Of these, 2 people (0.2%) would actually have COVID-19 (false negative result).\n If 1000 people with no symptoms for COVID-19 were tested for IgG antibodies and 500 (50%) of them had previously had COVID-19 infection more than 21 days previously:\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-10",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Failure of diagnostic tests to detect infection with COVID-19 when it is present (a false negative result) may delay treatment and risk further spread of infection to others. ",
          "start": 1059,
          "end": 1234,
          "context": "Accurate testing allows identification of people who need to isolate themselves to prevent the spread of infection, or who might need treatment for their infection.  Failure of diagnostic tests to detect infection with COVID-19 when it is present (a false negative result) may delay treatment and risk further spread of infection to others.  Incorrect diagnosis of COVID-19 when it is not present (a false positive result) may lead to unnecessary further testing, treatment, and isolation of the person and close contacts. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd::sentence-10",
          "documentId": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd",
          "text": "That means the virus could still spread.\n",
          "start": 1623,
          "end": 1664,
          "context": "In a Facebook Live conversation with New Jersey Gov. Phil Murphy, Fauci said the coronavirus vaccine will not be 100% effective and won’t be taken by the entire population.  That means the virus could still spread.\n \"So when a vaccine comes, we look at it as an important tool to supplement the public health measures that we do,” he said. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd",
          "text": "He and other health care professionals have observed that the Southern Hemisphere has had a very light flu season, probably because measures to curb the coronavirus, such as social distancing and mask-wearing, have limited the spread of the flu.\n",
          "start": 811,
          "end": 1057,
          "context": "Flu shots are an important measure to help the U.S. get through the winter, he said.\n He and other health care professionals have observed that the Southern Hemisphere has had a very light flu season, probably because measures to curb the coronavirus, such as social distancing and mask-wearing, have limited the spread of the flu.\n \"If we listen to the public health measures, not only would we diminish the effect of COVID-19, we might get away with a very, very light flu season if we combine that with getting the flu vaccine,\" Fauci said.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd",
          "text": "Sept. 25, 2020 -- Anthony Fauci, MD, says talk about a second wave of the coronavirus is premature because the United States is still dealing with the first one.\n",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 162,
          "context": "Sept. 25, 2020 -- Anthony Fauci, MD, says talk about a second wave of the coronavirus is premature because the United States is still dealing with the first one.\n The idea of a second wave is based on the 1918 flu pandemic, when many cases were seen in the spring, he says. "
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "atomId": "atom-2",
      "spans": [
        {
